+# Secure Web Dashboard with Nginx, HTTP Basic Auth, Let's Encrypt, and Systemd
+
+## Overview
+This skill provides a repeatable process to expose a local web dashboard (e.g., Hermes Agent dashboard on port 9119) via a domain name with:
+- HTTPS using Let's Encrypt (certbot)
+- HTTP Basic Auth protection
+- Correct Host‑header forwarding to avoid “Invalid Host header” errors
+- Systemd service for the dashboard to survive reboots
+- Nginx configuration that forwards the Authorization header so the browser only prompts once per session
+
+## When to Use
+- You have a locally‑bound web service (e.g., `hermes dashboard --port 9119 --host 127.0.0.1`)
+- You own a domain pointing to the server’s IP
+- You want secure, password‑protected access via HTTPS
+
+## Prerequisites
+- Root or sudo access on a Ubuntu/Debian‑like system
+- `hermes` CLI installed and available in PATH
+- Domain DNS A record pointing to the server’s public IP
+- Ports 80 and 443 open in firewall (ufw allow 80/tcp; ufw allow 443/tcp)
+- (Optional) A working email for Let's Encrypt notifications
+
+## Procedure
+
+### 1. Install required packages
+```bash
+sudo apt update
+sudo apt install -y nginx certbot python3-certbot-nginx apache2-utils
+```
+*`apache2-utils` provides `htpasswd` for HTTP Basic Auth.*
+
+### 2. Create HTTP Basic Auth credentials\nChoose a username and password (the user in this session prefers `chrisspeaks:Cris0037`):\n```bash\nsudo htpasswd -c -b /etc/nginx/.htpasswd <username> <password>\n```\n*Example:* `sudo htpasswd -c -b /etc/nginx/.htpasswd chrisspeaks Cris0037`
+
+### 3. Ensure the dashboard runs locally and is reachable
+Start the dashboard on the desired port (default 9119) and host 127.0.0.1:
+```bash
+hermes dashboard --port
+```
+
+```markdown
+ 9119 --host 127.0.0.1 &
+```
+*Note:* For persistence, see step 6 (Systemd service).
+
+Verify locally:
+```bash
+curl -s http://127.0.0.1:9119 | head -5
+```
+Should return HTML (e.g., `<!doctype html>`).
+
+### 4. Obtain/renew Let's Encrypt certificate
+Run Certbot with the Nginx plugin (non‑interactive):
+```bash
+sudo certbot --nginx -d <your.domain> --non-interactive --agree-tos --email <your.email>
+```
+Replace `<your.domain>` (e.g., `agent.miniprojectlab.com`) and `<your.email>` (e.g., `chrisvinclaw@gmail.com`).
+
+Certbot will:
+- Obtain a certificate
+- Modify `/etc/nginx/sites-available/<your.domain>` (or create a new one)
+- Set up automatic renewal via a systemd timer
+
+### 5. Configure Nginx reverse proxy with proper headers
+Edit (or create) the site configuration at `/etc/nginx/sites-available/<your.domain>`:
+
+```nginx
+# HTTP -> HTTPS redirect
+server {
+    listen 80;
+    server_name <your.domain>;
+    return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
+}
+
+server {
+    listen 443 ssl;
+    server_name <your.domain>;
+
+    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/<your.domain>/fullchain.pem;
+    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/<your.domain>/privkey.pem;
+
+    auth_basic "Restricted Access";
+    auth_basic_user_file /etc/nginx/.htpasswd;
+
+    location / {
+        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:9119;
+        proxy_http_version 1.1;
+        # Forward the original Host header (important for logging/redirects)
+        proxy_set_header Host $host;
+        # Correct Host header for the backend (prevents “Invalid Host header”)
+        proxy_set_header Host 127.0.0.1:9119;
+        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
+        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
+        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
+        # Forward Authorization so backend sees credentials (prevents repeated prompts)
+        proxy_set_header Authorization $http_authorization;
+        # A
+```
+
+```markdown
+void hop‑by‑boundary issues
+        proxy_set_header Connection "";
+        proxy_set_header Proxy-Connection "";
+    }
+}
+```
+
+*Key points:*
+- `proxy_set_header Host $host;` keeps the original hostname for logging/redirects.
+- `proxy_set_header Host 127.0.0.1:9119;` (or the line above) is required by the Hermes dashboard to validate the Host header.
+- `proxy_set_header Authorization $http_authorization;` ensures the browser’s Basic Auth token is sent to the backend, so the dashboard’s XHR calls don’t trigger a new login prompt.
+- Empty `Connection` and `Proxy-Connection` headers prevent nginx from hop‑by‑boundary interference.
+
+Test the configuration:
+```bash
+sudo nginx -t
+```
+If successful, reload Nginx:
+```bash
+sudo systemctl reload nginx
+```
+
+### 6. Create a Systemd service for the dashboard (persistent across reboots)
+Create `/etc/systemd/system/hermes-dashboard.service`:
+
+```ini
+[Unit]
+Description=Hermes Agent Dashboard
+After=network.target
+
+[Service]
+Type=simple
+User=root                     # or create a dedicated non‑root user
+WorkingDirectory=/root
+ExecStart=/usr/local/lib/hermes-agent/venv/bin/hermes dashboard --port 9119 --host 127.0.0.1
+Restart=on-failure
+RestartSec=5
+# Optional: set environment variables if needed
+# Environment=HERMES_INFERENCE_MODEL=nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free
+# Environment=HERMES_INFERENCE_PROVIDER=openrouter
+
+[Install]
+WantedBy=multi-user.target
+```
+
+Enable and start the service:
+```bash
+sudo systemctl daemon-reload
+sudo systemctl enable --now hermes-dashboard.service
+```
+
+Check status:
+```bash
+sudo systemctl status hermes-dashboard.service
+```
+
+Logs are available via:
+```bash
+journalctl -u hermes-dashboard -f
+```
+
+### 7. Final verification
+From a browser, navigate to `https://<your.domain>`:
+- You should see a login dialog (username/password from step 2).
+- After successful login, the Hermes dashboard loads.
+- Subsequent re
+```
+
+```markdown
+quests within the same browser session should **not** re‑prompt for credentials (thanks to the Authorization header forwarding).
+- The certificate should be trusted (Let’s Encrypt).
+
+You can also test with curl:
+```bash
+curl -k -u <username>:<password> https://<your.domain>/api/status
+```
+Should return a JSON payload (e.g., version, gateway state).
+
+## Pitfalls & Troubleshooting
+| Symptom | Likely Cause | Fix |
+|---------|--------------|-----|
+| **502 Bad Gateway** | Dashboard not listening on 127.0.0.1:9119 | Verify dashboard process (`ps aux | grep hermes dashboard`). Start/restart via systemd. |
+| **502 Bad Gateway after Hermes gateway restart/update** | Dashboard service not running or not enabled | Ensure hermes-dashboard service is enabled and running: `systemctl enable --now hermes-dashboard.service` |
+| **Invalid Host header** | Nginx not sending correct Host header to backend | Ensure `proxy_set_header Host 127.0.0.1:9119;` (or `$host` plus proper backend config) is present. |
+| **Repeated login prompts** | Authorization header not forwarded to backend | Add `proxy_set_header Authorization $http_authorization;` in the location block. |
+| **Certificate mismatch / browser warning** | Using self‑signed cert instead of Let’s Encrypt | Run Certbot again or ensure the site config points to `/etc/letsencrypt/live/<domain>/...`. |
+| **Service fails to start** | Wrong path to `hermes` binary or missing env vars | Check `ExecStart` path with `which hermes`. Adjust User/WorkingDirectory if needed. |
+| **Port already in use** | Another process bound to 9119 | `ss -tlnp | grep 9119` and kill the conflicting process, or change the dashboard port and update Nginx accordingly. |
+
+## Maintenance
+- **Certificate renewal**: Handled automatically by Certbot’s timer (`systemctl list-timers | grep certbot`). Test with `sudo certbot renew --dry-run`.
+- **Update credentials**: Re-ru
+```
+
+```markdown
+n `htpasswd` (without `-c` to avoid overwriting): `sudo htpasswd -b /etc/nginx/.htpasswd <username> <newpassword>`.
+- **Dashboard updates**: Pull latest Hermes version (`hermes update`) and restart the service: `sudo systemctl restart hermes-dashboard`.
+- **Log rotation**: Nginx logs rotate via `logrotate`; dashboard logs via journald.
+
+## References
+- Nginx proxy documentation: <https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html>
+- Certbot Nginx plugin: <https://certbot.eff.org/instructions>
+- HTTP Basic Auth security note: credentials are base64‑encoded; always use HTTPS (provided by Let’s Encrypt).
+- Systemd service examples: <https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/systemd.service.html>
+
+```

